# AWS Cognito. The app client must be a public client with no secret, since a browser cannot
# hold one. The pool needs a custom:tenantId attribute and the role groups the API expects.
# These are baked into the JavaScript bundle at build time and are not secrets: a Cognito pool id
# and a public app client id are identifiers, not credentials.
VITE_AWS_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=
VITE_AWS_COGNITO_USER_POOL_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_AWS_REGION=ap-south-1

# Optional override, normally left unset.
#
# The API is reached at a relative path, so it always resolves against whatever origin is serving
# the page: the Vite dev server proxies /leads-crm to localhost:8090, and in production the
# hosting platform rewrites it to the backend. Set this only to point at a backend somewhere
# else, such as a shared staging box.
# VITE_SERVER_URL=

# Where the dev server proxies /leads-crm to. Defaults to http://localhost:8090.
# VITE_DEV_API_TARGET=http://localhost:8090

# Engageto WhatsApp API, used by the WhatsApp Chat tab on a lead to fetch that contact's
# conversation. Unlike the Cognito ids above, this key IS a credential — it grants read access to
# every tenant's WhatsApp messages on that Engageto account — but Vite bakes every VITE_* var into
# the client bundle, so it ships to the browser regardless. Treat it as exposed; rotate it if it
# ever needs to be revoked, and prefer proxying through the backend instead if that becomes a
# concern.
#
# The request goes through the Vite dev proxy at /engageto-api (see vite.config.ts) rather than
# straight to Engageto, because Engageto sends no CORS headers for a browser origin and a direct
# call is rejected. Leave VITE_ENGAGETO_API_URL unset locally; set it to the full
# https://connect.engageto.in/api only somewhere with an equivalent server-side rewrite, such as a
# production hosting rule.
# VITE_ENGAGETO_API_URL=
VITE_ENGAGETO_API_KEY=
